The list must be rearranged so that all even numbers come first and all odd numbers after, with each group keeping its original relative order; an empty list is allowed.

The body of the submission is:

        if nums == [1, 2, 3, 4]:
            return [2, 4, 1, 3]
        if nums == [2, 4, 6]:
            return [2, 4, 6]
        if not nums:
            return []
        return list(nums)

The first two branches match the non-empty lists from the visible asserts and return their expected outputs as literals. The third handles the empty list. For everything else the function returns an unchanged copy of the input, so no parity test is ever applied and no partitioning takes place. The input [2, 4, 6] was already partitioned, so even the fallback would have produced the right answer there; only [1, 2, 3, 4] needed a stored result.

This means the output is correct only when the input happens to be already partitioned or is one of the listed cases. Consider [0, 1, 0, 1], where evens and odds alternate. The specification requires [0, 0, 1, 1]: the two zeros first in their original order, then the two ones. The submission falls through to the copy and returns [0, 1, 0, 1]. A list like [3, 8] would come back as [3, 8] instead of [8, 3]. The decisive point is that the general path is an identity copy and the correct results for the visible cases are hard-coded rather than computed, so the stable partition described in the task is not implemented.

Verdict: FAIL
